        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "57d86194-694e-3f6a-bf93-f2e898e816b5",
                "word": "phénoménologique",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "46fc547d-a856-43be-9245-726bf1c888b2",
                        "entry_id": "57d86194-694e-3f6a-bf93-f2e898e816b5",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "phénoménologiques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                        "entry_id": "57d86194-694e-3f6a-bf93-f2e898e816b5",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relatif à la phénoménologie ; d'après les méthodes de la phénoménologie."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "41a4b56b-0eae-4a5d-8172-6601365ba4d0",
                                "sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                                "text": "[…] on se souvient notamment d'un reportage d'anthologie de Marguerite Duras qui prétendait savoir qui était l'assassin du petit Grégory par une étrange méthode phénoménologique dont elle avait seule le secret, et qui n'a pas beaucoup aidé les enquêteurs ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "258e3b65-74ea-4de4-8fb6-f3edd29693f9",
                                "sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                                "text": "Ainsi, l’anthropologie phénoménologique de Blumenberg n’est-elle pas la présentation d’un « savoir », c’est plutôt la construction d’un ensemble d’hypothèses sur l’homme qui impliquent un va-et-vient avec l’empirique, et qui ne se « clôture » pas."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fcdd9f4f-0b60-4f1e-bb85-c555b1b09a1f",
                                "sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                                "text": "Or, pour Schutz il semble bien y avoir au cœur de la démarche phénoménologique tout un domaine inutile au chercheur en sciences sociales qui peut dès lors simplement l'ignorer, le jugeant sans utilité pour son investigation […]."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5c43dcb1-7ca0-4a9f-9aa2-1d87a26aab58",
                        "source_entry_id": "57d86194-694e-3f6a-bf93-f2e898e816b5",
                        "source_sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                        "target_lang_word": "phenomenological",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "phenomenological",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                        "entry_id": "9410744e-948a-3762-9dd5-2409544e0235",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to phenomenology, or consistent with the principles of phenomenology."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cdea9edd-05eb-4e16-8746-cb333d83a491",
                                                "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                                "text": "Phenomenological \"things\" are not commonsense objects or sense data but the phenomena in their presentation, grasped as intentional objects."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "230779bb-fcf8-44d9-afff-69278f0f6fa4",
                                                "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                                "text": "I call my models \"mechanistic\" to distinguish them from classical models that are more phenomenological."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "22055b45-bb80-4e26-885b-d448f006d944",
                                                "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                                "text": "A similar and more influential use of the term can be found in William Whewell's Philosophy of the Inductive Sciences (1847), where phenomenology occurs in the context of the \"palaetiological sciences\" (i.e., sciences which deal wih more ancient conditions of things), as that branch of these studies which is to be followed by aetiology and theory. Among such phenomenologies Whewell mentions particularly phenomenological uranology, phenomenological geography of plants and animals, and even a phenomenological glossology."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06e8dd0f-9a4d-4faa-8f89-692958598649",
                                                "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                                "text": "The far-flungness of the word, the phenomenological pleasure of finding it variously transformed by Ransom's modernity and Beowulf’s venerability made me feel vaguely something for which again I only found the words years later."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9e4e92ba-0cfd-4f31-921a-3d30092e6dc2",
                                                "sense_id": "276bae33-139e-381f-af1b-8f6018ec3da8",
                                                "text": "He [Martin Heidegger] was influenced by Edmund Husserl, a German thinker born in 1859 who was soon to become the leading figure of the phenomenological movement, dedicated to the description and investigation of our conscious experience without reference to its extramental causes and consequences."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "448f5173-f8f7-34ea-a596-ae5d6acacf94",
                                        "entry_id": "9410744e-948a-3762-9dd5-2409544e0235",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Using the method of phenomenology, by which the observer examines data and other subjective effects without trying to provide a pathophysiological explanation of them, especially in diagnosing disease states and in nosology and other forms of taxonomy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c4b41b01-fb5f-469e-bb81-d91184b42a06",
                                                "sense_id": "448f5173-f8f7-34ea-a596-ae5d6acacf94",
                                                "text": "Ross and his colleagues … drew on prior research...to suggest that addictive gambling resembles dependence on stimulants (like cocaine) more than it does alcoholism, and hence enlarges our understanding of addiction more fully than purely behavioural criteria would do. The worry is that a behavioural approach misses the similarities and differences between forms of addiction by treating all as more or less the same, based on shared behavioural and phenomenological effects."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5c4e62bb-c606-4fe9-babc-5b931ca9a557",
                        "source_entry_id": "57d86194-694e-3f6a-bf93-f2e898e816b5",
                        "source_sense_id": "2e301f3b-52dc-3465-9660-bd0102127dec",
                        "target_lang_word": "fenomenologico",
                        "target_lang_code": "it",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "fenomenologico",
                                "target_lang_code": "it",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a5db4873-a062-38b7-8c38-065d759efa1b",
                                        "entry_id": "f1c1e838-9bfa-354b-b1be-ed0b33e33aa0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "nella filosofia di Hegel è tutto quanto rientra nel principio \\\"teorico-filosofico\\\" che rispetta l'ordine logico-razionale secondo la verità di una tesi, un'antitesi ed una \\\"soluzione\\\"... le medesime rispettando tale principio sia nel loro legame sia approfondite ognuna singolarmente infine \\\"diramandosi\\\" in categorie differenti"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "17ba240d-4b64-4859-a54d-13e495e0b830",
                                                "sense_id": "a5db4873-a062-38b7-8c38-065d759efa1b",
                                                "text": "\"tutto ciò che è razionale è logico e tutto quanto è logico... razionale\" è una sorta di teorema filosofico dell'aspetto fenomenologico del reale"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
